// Building types. kind sets what the building does: town_hall (stores goods, houses people, trains
// workers and researches techs), storehouse, house, farm, smelter, barracks, wall (solid to wallHeight, laid
// in lines a column at a time, and put right next to other walls), gate (a wall whose bottom two levels
// are gate leaves that only its owner's units walk through) or tower (solid to wallHeight across its whole
// footprint, put up alone or onto the player's own walls, which become part of it). size is [x, z] in blocks
// (the door is on the +z side); wallHeight is in blocks; wall, footing (the bottom course, if different)
// and roof are building materials: planks, thatch, roof_tile, stone_brick, palisade (a barrier's stakes),
// rampart (dressed stone drawn with battlements) or trestle (a watchtower's timber frame under a railed
// deck); chimney adds a stone chimney.
// Buildings come in tiers. upgrade (optional; not lights, totems or bridges) names the building this one is
// turned into where it stands, for that building's cost and buildTime: a building named as an upgrade is never
// laid as it is, only reached by upgrading. An upgrade is of the same kind and at least as tall; a wall, gate
// or tower keeps its size, and anything else may be bigger: it grows from its middle onto free, level ground
// round it. speed (optional, 100 if not given) is how fast it trains units and smelts, in percent. facing (optional, an upgrade one column across) names the building
// that goes up on each free side of this one when its upgrade is finished, so the wall is three columns thick:
// the upgrade's cost covers it, and a facing's own cost is what it takes to put a lost one back. A facing is
// of the same kind and height, and is never laid as it is. sturdy (optional, 1 if not given) is how many times
// the damage of the same block elsewhere each of its blocks takes before it is knocked out. thick (optional,
// walls, gates and towers) marks a thick, solid one: blows struck at it close up do "thick" times less (see
// roles.json), and a blast (see units.json) that knocks a block out of it topples what stands above and beside
// it away from the blast, onto whoever is behind. walkway (optional,
// walls, gates and towers): ranged units can be posted on top of it, along its edges. aim (optional, towers
// with a walkway): of the shots of those posted on it that would miss in the dark or bad weather, aim in a
// hundred land after all, and a fifth of aim more in a hundred of their hits land on the head. A tower sees
// far: give it a large sight.
// buildTime is the seconds one worker takes; cost is in goods; housing is the people it houses; storage
// means goods are delivered and kept there; raidable means enemies can capture or burn it; workerSlots is
// how many workers can work a farm or smelter at once; sight is how far (columns) beyond its walls its
// player sees under fog of war (6 if not given); requires names the tech (or list of techs) the player must
// have researched first; faction (optional) limits it to one faction; icon (optional, the id if not given)
// names the picture the game shows for it (see game/scripts/UiIcons.cs). light (optional) is how far (columns
// beyond its walls) its lantern or fire lights the ground from dusk to dawn, so what stands there is seen from
// the daytime distance and shot at as by day; kind light is a torch post or campfire, a solid column of its
// wall material wallHeight high (see src/Sim/Conditions.cs). Kind totem is a city-state's totem, which nobody
// builds (see citystates.json): a plinth of its footing across the footprint with a pole of its wall material
// in the middle, wallHeight high in all.
// fromDen (optional): built only on a cleared monster den, by a faction whose prize there names it ("prizes" in units.json),
// never from the build menu. yield and yieldTime (optional, together): goods it brings in on its own every
// yieldTime seconds once finished, into its owner's storage nearest it.
// Kind quarry is a quarry or mine: workers work it like a farm (up to workerSlots at once) and never run it dry.
// works is what a round of work gives and workTime the seconds a round takes (both needed, and only for a
// quarry); with several goods, each worker's rounds give them in turn (iron_ore 4 then coal 2, and again), so
// the list sets both which goods and how much of each. Gathering techs make the rounds quicker.
// Kind shrine: its worker (up to workerSlots) brings it crystal from storage, and it channels each crystal
// into mana for its owner, channelTime seconds a crystal (at its speed). Spells cost mana. mana (a shrine
// only, needed) is the mana one crystal gives.
// shore: true means it must stand at the water's edge, with open water in a column right beside its footprint
// (the fishing hut, a quarry whose rounds give food).
// Kind great_work is a faction's great work (see "Ascendancy" in docs/rules.md): it stands only in the deep caves,
// on ground under the land, a player has one at a time, and once finished it wins the match for its owner if it
// is still standing a few minutes later.
